As covered in[Visual Regressions and How to Prevent Them](https://meticulous.ai/blog/visual-regressions-and-how-to-prevent-them/) , visual regression tests are a faster way to preserve your application’s health.


While SaaS solutions provide advanced visual regression and project management features, this article will focus on how to implement simple yet effective[visual regression tests](https://www.meticulous.ai/blog/the-beginner-guide-to-visual-regression-testing) using[Jest](https://www.meticulous.ai/blog/how-to-use-jest-spyon) and[Cypress](https://www.meticulous.ai/blog/cypress-testing) .


We will set up visual regression tests on an example[repository](https://github.com/charlypoly/visual-regression-testing-examples) , with the following component:


```text
const Avatar = ({ src, className }) => {
const [fallback, setFallback] = React.useState(false);


const onFallback = React.useCallback((e) => setFallback(true), []);


return (
<div className={className}>
{fallback ? <AvatarFallback /> : <img src={src} onError={onFallback} />}
</div>
);
};


export default styled(Avatar)`
margin: 10px;


& img {
border-radius: 50%;
}


```


Our[component](https://github.com/charlypoly/visual-regression-testing-examples/blob/master/src/Avatar.jsx) displays the given image with some styling, and falls back to a custom component if the image fails to load.


We will set up both snapshot and screen testing for this repository with integration at the Pull Request level.


## Set up Snapshot Tests


Most snapshot tests setups rely on[Jest](https://jestjs.io/) (a[JavaScript](https://www.meticulous.ai/blog/javascript-ui-testing-best-practices) Testing Framework), which provides a set of utilities to perform snapshot comparison.


Given a test scenario mounting a component, Jest will do an initial snapshot of the rendered DOM tree. Future runs of the same tests will trigger snapshot comparisons that will fail if a difference is found.


## Installation and Configuration


We need first to install the required dependencies:


```text
yarn add jest @testing-library/jest-dom @testing-library/react @testing-library/user-event react-test-renderer


```


Then, we need to add some entries in our[package.json](https://github.com/charlypoly/visual-regression-testing-examples/blob/master/package.json) :


```text
/* … */
"scripts": {
"test-snapshot": "react-scripts test",
/* … */
},
"jest": {
"testMatch": [
"**/?(*.)+(snapshot).(spec|test).[jt]s?(x)"
]
},
/* … */


```


The jest entry contains the[Jest configuration](https://jestjs.io/docs/configuration) .


Since the example project is built with[Create React App](https://create-react-app.dev/) , we extend the existing Jest configuration by specifying the test files matching pattern with testMatch.


In our setup, we can run tests by calling react-scripts test. Without CRA, simply running the jest command as follows will run the tests:


```text
/* … */
"scripts": {
"test-snapshot": "jest",
/* … */
},
/* … */


```


## Writing and Running our Tests


We can now write our snapshot test in a new src/__tests__/snapshot/Avatar.jsx[file](https://github.com/charlypoly/visual-regression-testing-examples/blob/master/src/__tests__/snapshot/Avatar.jsx) :


```text
import React from "react";
import renderer from "react-test-renderer";
import Avatar from "../src/Avatar";


describe("<Avatar />", () => {
describe("with a valid image", () => {
it("renders correctly", () => {
const tree = renderer
.create(
<Avatar
src={
"https://i.picsum.photos/id/623/200/200.jpg?hmac=xquTjHIYmAPV3XEGlIUaV_KWyEofkbortxrK79jJhWA"
}
/>
)
.toJSON();
expect(tree).toMatchSnapshot();
});
});
describe("with an invalid image", () => {
it("renders correctly", async () => {
const tree = renderer.create(<Avatar src={"<not_and_url>"} />).toJSON();
expect(tree).toMatchSnapshot();
});
});
});


```


The renderer() function from react-test-renderer helps render the given component and serialize the generated DOM tree as JSON.


Then, the DOM result is passed to Jest expectation matcher toMatchSnapshot(), creating a snapshot file and raising a failure if a diff is detected with an existing snapshot.


We can now run our test with the yarn test-snapshot command:


A src/__snapshots__/Avatar.snapshot.spec.jsx.snap file is created.


This snapshot file should be committed since it will be used as a reference snapshot for further tests (locally and on CI).


## Setup the CI Workflow


Our test setup is working locally. However, to ensure that no visual regression is introduced in our component, we must enforce those tests when new Pull Requests are created on Github.


Let’s create a new[Github Action](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/learn-github-actions) to enforce such a policy by creating a Workflow file. Github Action Workflow files are a[broad subject to cover](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/using-workflows/workflow-syntax-for-github-actions) ; however, let’s have an overview of the main parts of the .github/workflows/snapshot-test.yml[file](https://github.com/charlypoly/visual-regression-testing-examples/blob/master/.github/workflows/snapshot-tests.yml) :


- We define a[strategy.matrix](https://github.com/charlypoly/visual-regression-testing-examples/blob/master/.github/workflows/snapshot-tests.yml#L12-L14) to run our tests on multiple versions of Node.js (12.x and 17.x)
- We configure[caches](https://github.com/charlypoly/visual-regression-testing-examples/blob/master/.github/workflows/snapshot-tests.yml#L25-L40) for yarn dependencies and Jest


Finally, we run the[tests](https://github.com/charlypoly/visual-regression-testing-examples/blob/master/.github/workflows/snapshot-tests.yml#L42) .


## Testing the Workflow


The following[Pull Request](https://github.com/charlypoly/visual-regression-testing-examples/pull/1) showcases how snapshots tests are integrated into the CI workflow.


This Pull Request introduces a breaking change by inverting the condition on the fallback mechanism.


Immediately, the[snapshots tests catch the visual regression](https://github.com/charlypoly/visual-regression-testing-examples/actions/runs/1897429449/jobs/2682953715) by reporting the DOM differences initiated by this new change:


*Note: If the Snapshot changes were intentional, simply running the test locally with the --updateSnapshot argument will update the snapshots with the new changes: yarn test-snapshot --updateSnapshot.*


*Once the updated snapshots are committed, the CI tests will pass.*


## Snapshot Test Limitations


Looking closely at our snapshot tests reveals two main limitations.


First, we can see in our[generated snapshot file (on master)](https://github.com/charlypoly/visual-regression-testing-examples/blob/master/src/__tests__/snapshot/__snapshots__/Avatar.jsx.snap) that the “ with an invalid image renders correctly" test case is not generating the proper snapshot:


```text
exports[`<Avatar /> with an invalid image renders correctly 1`] = `
<div
className="sc-bdvvtL oSqWo"
>
<img
onError={[Function]}
src="<not_and_url>"
/>
</div>
`;


```


This first limitation is generally linked to the difficulty to test image loading scenarios in Jest tests.


To properly test a “failing to load image” scenario with Jest,[we will need to mock many parts of the code to trigger such a scenario](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/44462665/how-do-you-use-jest-to-test-img-onerror) . On the other hand, screen tests by running headless browser instances could help in testing such features.


Finally, any breaking change at the styling level raises cryptic snapshots changes, as shown in this[Pull Request](https://github.com/charlypoly/visual-regression-testing-examples/pull/2) :


The resulting snapshot test error is not very helpful in understanding which change introduced the issues, or what the final visual regression for end-users is.


While some Jest plugins such as[jest-styled-components](https://github.com/styled-components/jest-styled-components) help deal with this issue, screen testing is more appropriate for assessing visual regression on UI-Kit components.


## Setup Screen Tests


As seen earlier, we need to confirm that our UI-Kit component has no visual regression of any kind, from styling to advanced DOM interactions (image failing load).


Based on actual screenshots of components' rendering, screen tests are the best way to avoid visual regressions of our component.


Screen tests share the same core logic as Snapshot tests, but by comparing screenshots.


Screen tests require[Cypress](https://www.cypress.io/) (a JavaScript End to End Testing Framework) to start a headless browser instance to perform screenshots of the rendered components.


## Installation and Configuration


The following libraries are required to perform screenshots tests on components:


- [Cypress Component testing](https://docs.cypress.io/guides/component-testing/overview) : a feature of Cypress that allows running test of components without having to set up a whole application (usually required for E2E testing)
- [cypress-image-snapshot](https://github.com/jaredpalmer/cypress-image-snapshot) : A Cypress plugin that brings the screenshot capabilities


Let’s install all the required dependencies:


```text
yarn add @cypress/react @cypress/webpack-dev-server cypress cypress-image-snapshot


```


Then,[creating a cypress.json file](https://github.com/charlypoly/visual-regression-testing-examples/blob/master/cypress.json) to specify test files pattern and location:


```text
{
"component": {
"componentFolder": "src",
"testFiles": "**/*.visual.spec.{js,jsx,ts,tsx}"
},
"video": false // Cypress will record videos by default
}


```


Again, we need to update our package.json to add a new script entry:


```text
// …
“scripts”: {
"test-visual": "cypress run-ct --reporter cypress-image-snapshot/reporter",
}
// …


```


Finally, we need to properly configure the[cypress-image-snapshot](https://github.com/jaredpalmer/cypress-image-snapshot) plugin by creating two files:


- [cypress/plugins/index.js](https://github.com/charlypoly/visual-regression-testing-examples/blob/master/cypress/plugins/index.js) : configures Cypress with our CRA webpack config and enables the[cypress-image-snapshot](https://github.com/jaredpalmer/cypress-image-snapshot) plugin
- [cypress/support/index.js](https://github.com/charlypoly/visual-regression-testing-examples/blob/master/cypress/support/index.js) : configures the[cypress-image-snapshot](https://github.com/jaredpalmer/cypress-image-snapshot) plugin options


## Writing and Running our Test


We can now define our[screen tests](https://github.com/charlypoly/visual-regression-testing-examples/blob/master/src/__tests__/screen/Avatar.jsx) as follows:


```text
/* eslint-disable no-undef */
import React from "react";
import { mount } from "@cypress/react";
import Avatar from "./Avatar";


describe("<Avatar />", () => {
describe("with a valid image", () => {
it("renders the proper UI", () => {
mount(
<Avatar
src={
}
/>
);
cy.matchImageSnapshot();
});
});
describe("with an invalid image", () => {
it("renders the proper UI", () => {
mount(<Avatar src={"<not_and_url>"} />);
cy.matchImageSnapshot();
});
});
});


```


The above tests are similar to previously seen Snapshot tests.


The only difference is that we mount the component and call an action on cy (cypress runner): cy.matchImageSnapshot().


Similar to Jest’s toMatchSnapshot() method, cy.matchImageSnapshot() will take a screenshot of the rendered component and raise an error if any difference is found with the existing one.


We can now run our screen test with the yarn test-visual command:


Our tests generate one screenshot file per test case (describe()) that must be committed.


## Setup the CI Workflow


Again, we need to integrate those tests in the development workflow by creating the corresponding Github Actions.


The new[.github/workflows/screen-tests.yml](https://github.com/charlypoly/visual-regression-testing-examples/blob/master/.github/workflows/screen-tests.yml) file contains the following parts:


- The[container parameter](https://github.com/charlypoly/visual-regression-testing-examples/blob/master/.github/workflows/screen-tests.yml#L11) initialize a Docker image provided by Cypress that includes all required operating system dependencies and some pre-installed browsers to run the tests
- The[“Store screenshots”](https://github.com/charlypoly/visual-regression-testing-examples/blob/master/.github/workflows/screen-tests.yml#L19-L23) action ensures that screenshots will be downloadable


